NIVO78 FAMILY IDENTITY — LOOK, FEEL, AND OPERATE THE
SAME

================================================================================



Shared visual system

- Every Nivo78 app uses the shared nivo78-theme
package — typography, color roles, spacing tokens, and component shapes — not
one-off styling per product.

- Density is utility-first: compact vertical rhythm,
scannable sections, no hero banners or decorative animation inside core
workflows.

- Primary actions use the same button hierarchy
everywhere: primary (solid), secondary (outlined/text), destructive (labeled
and confirmed).

- Page titles follow one pattern: destination name,
optional subtitle in muted body text — no marketing headlines on form screens.

- Status and progress use the same vocabulary and
colors: not started / needs attention / complete (or product-equivalent labels
mapped to those three states).

- Icons and affordances are consistent: ? opens Help
for that field or task; never a mystery icon with no label.



Shared navigation chrome

- Desktop (APP-DESKTOP-MODERN-STYLE — reference/NIVO78_DESKTOP_MENU_BAR.txt):
  - Standard top menu on every PC/Mac window: File (at least Exit), product menus, Help in canonical order.
  - Optional action toolbar below the header for high-frequency commands only; MUST NOT duplicate top-menu commands (no parallel File/Help on the bar).
  - Keyboard shortcuts wired to menu commands; context menus for row/selection edits; ~80px logo header; accent stripe on primary bar buttons when a bar is used.

- Mobile: a visible support row or drawer section with
the same five links; never bury Privacy or Terms only on the website.

- Back navigation preserves unsaved form state until
the user saves or explicitly cancels/reverts — except when Save failed
validation: Back, Cancel, and discard navigation stay blocked until items are fixed
or the user chooses Save anyway on acknowledgement warnings.

- Current location is obvious: active tab, breadcrumb,
or page title matches engine destination id (useful for diagnostics and Help
deep links).



Shared page structure

- Top: page title + optional one-line intro (plain
language, no paragraph essays).

- Body: grouped sections with short section headers;
fields in consistent layout per form factor (see Mobile phone input chrome —
phone-only).

- Bottom (when editing): save/cancel bar in the same
place and behavior across apps — visible when dirty, disabled when invalid,
never hidden until the user discovers a gesture.

- Critical warnings stay inline near the control they
protect; everything else goes behind ?.



